Subject: AIDS, Female Math, and Spaghetti w/ Clam Sauce

As I recall, Stanton McAndlish asked if anyone's aware of any credible
support for the claim that HIV doesn't cause AIDS.  This subject has come
up before, and I seem to be the one most familiar with this argument - but
I'd like it if anyone was more expert on the subject so I could learn from
them.

The simple answer is: yes.  Dr. Peter Duesberg's published, peer-reviewed
papers poke big gaping holes in the hypothesis that HIV causes AIDS which
haven't been overcome by anyone.  All anyone does to defend it is to
point out the correlation of AIDS with HIV, which is by definition (AIDS
is defined as any one or more of about 25 diseases in the presence of HIV)
and also relies upon presumptive diagnosis of HIV in many PWAs (40% of all
PWAs in 1987 - dunno what the current number is).

The more complex answer is that a respectable alternative hypothesis has
emerged: the co-factor speculation.  People like Luc Montangier, the
discoverer of HIV, have concluded that HIV isn't the "sole causative
agent" (in other words, the only cause) of AIDS.  They have no idea what
else does, but would really like it if grants were distributed for
research to try to find out what it could be - the National Institute of
Health doesn't do so for any research that deviates from the HIV-AIDS
orthodoxy.

There's lots of research to back this up.  HIV can't even be found in
most of the semen of HIV+ men, so how could this be the way AIDS is
transmitted?  In PWAs, HIV destroys T-cells slower than they are regener-
ated.  There's never been any controlled study which supports the HIV-
AIDS hypothesis.

HIV and AIDS have also been fairly stable epidemiologically in the USA.
about 1 million people have had HIV since testing for it first began.
This number hasn't changed, like a new, spreading virus normally does.
This is the behavior of an old virus that has reached a sort of equilibrium
instead.  Similarly, despite claims that heterosexuals would start
coming down with AIDS RSN, we haven't.  The high-risk groups for AIDS
have remained IV drug users and homosexuals.  Those of us outside those
groups have as much risk of getting AIDS as we do dying in a car accident
driving a few hours to and from a sexual encounter (that is, that's how
much risk we have of getting AIDS from a single sexual encounter).

You'll notice, if you observe the scare stories about HIV that whenever
they say that it's on the increase, they cite worldwide figures, not US
ones.  This reflects the reporting of HIV diagnoses in Africa and
southeast Asia more than anything else.  African "AIDS" was the subject
of Tony Brown's Journal, a PBS show devoted to black affairs from a
sort of "Harlem Renaissance" perspective, recently.  Celia Farber, a
writer for Spin magazine, told about her recent investigative trip to
Africa, which she apparently wrote a couple of article for Spin about.
Her findings don't support the conventional African AIDS story.

An engineer of some sort told her about setting up a public water system
in one village (either Kenyan or Nigerian, I can't remember which) that
had many of the symptoms of Slim disease, one of the AIDS diseases.  After
he finished a water system that differentiated between sewage and drinking
water, the symptoms disappeared.  Placebo effect occurred: people told
they had HIV started dying of AIDS, then stopped and went into remission
when told they didn't have HIV.  Also, people told they had HIV acted as
if they'd been condemned to death, had lots of unsafe sex, and contracted
many other sexually transmitted diseases instead.  The only pathology
recognizable as AIDS occurred in Ghanian prostitutes that had heroin and
crack habits.

As for the bit about the math test curves of boys and girls not even
overlapping, I presume that this means that those females that actually
are better than males at math just aren't numerous enough to be statistically
significant.

As for the Spaghetti w/ clam sauce, from all the discussion of nutrition
and diet going on, this seems to be the healthiest dish I like.  What do
the rest of you think?  The main problem I've had with improving my diet
is that I really don't like vegetables very much.  I hate beans, tofu,
uncooked greens - no matter how well they're prepared.  You could make the
best veggie chili in the world, and I'd hate it.  I like meat.  Steak,
fish, poultry, pork, venison, yum!

I did cut my milk intake almost completely when I took up singing, and have
noticed improvements in my speaking voice (even when I haven't been doing
much singing) and my respiratory system.  I used to sneeze, wheeze, and
cough a lot, and I don't anymore.  But all I did was stop drinking it.
I still eat cheese pizza, but not when I have a nose cold.  I don't eat
ice cream anymore, but I never did, much.  All I liked was vanilla,
anyways.  And all my relatives are dairy farmers - milk is a religion to
them!

