I have a physics question; actually a series of questions.
Only because I seem unable to receive satisfaction from
the physicists I know do I post here.  It seems to me that
it should have a rather simple answer.  If the replies
help me understand the issues involved, I will write an
essay summarizing the prerequisites and issues, in the
honored tradition of giving something back for others'
help.

The scenario:  Imagine a piece of the universe devoid of
matter except for two hydrogen atoms, approaching each
other.  Each has a single electron.  As they get nearer,
the electrons' energy changes. (Note I am not wondering
about conservation laws, I'm perfectly happy with that;
I'm curious about mechanism).

1. How is this change in energy "transmitted"?  I presume
that it is via photons, which are distinct entities.  These
might be "virtual photons".

2. What equation do I use to figure out the probability of
a photon being transmitted between these atoms (causing
the change in energy)?  I was told by one physicist that
the probability actually is ZERO at some distance, but
that doesn't seem right to me.  I'd guess that it simply
falls off to nearly zero rather quickly.  Similarly, what
formulae do I use to figure out the energy quanta that are
transferred?

3. Again similarly, as they get closer and the orbitals deform
much more, how many photon transfers take place, and what is
their energy?  What theory do I apply to calculate this?

That's enough for now.  If you feel like answering, that'd
be great!

