Eric S. Raymond says:
> > Is this true?  There were, I seem to recall, some serious questions as to
> > why the French on the one hand and the Inuit on the other had much lower
> > rates of heart disease than one might expect, given their high intakes of
> > fatty foods.
> 
> No.  But don't bother arguing, because logic and evidence won't be heard.
> Vegetarianism in the modern West has almost nothing to do with even the little
> we know about nutrition.  As the self-righteous tone of the postings here
> makes clear, it's essentially a religious movement --- a form of expiatory
> secular asceticism for people with too much sense to join a cult.

You are inaccurate on all points, Eric.

I am not an ascetic. I'm a hedonist. I assure you that I likely eat
better and tastier food than you do, and spend a lot more on it.

There also IS plenty of nutritional evidence for my position. Denying
its existance is, of course, perfectly possible. Feel free. Anyone
else, however, is welcome to look at the literature -- there are
plenty of references. Hell, Dean Ornish wasn't even the first fellow
to conduct controlled experiments that a low-fat vegan diet stops and
reverses heart disease -- although his is likely the most famous.
I remember a few months ago when Eric informed us all that if you were
a vegan you were likely to have all sorts of nutritional problems --
an assertion he backed with a single piece of anecdotal evidence,
never mind that there are literally millions of people on the planet
who live vegan lifestyles their whole lives without even a lick of
symptoms of nutrient deficiency (see, for instance, the Jains). I'd
say that Eric is the person here who doesn't follow evidence in his
conclusions, not the vegans on the list.

If you want to be anecdotal, I've got a cholesterol level just above
90, low triglicerides levels, a fabulous LDL/HDL ratio, excellent
blood pressure, I'm thin, and I never get sick any more. These are not
characteristics of me from the days when I ate steak sandwiches ever
day at lunch. If you want to look at controlled studies, do so --
there are plenty of them. If you want to argue about anomalies like
the French or the Italians or the Inuit, fine -- but do so knowing
that they are only minor deviations from the curve, not folks with
ultra-healthy hearts that somehow gorge lard all day long.

Eric has contended that logic and evidence won't be heard. Well, I was
I die-hard meat eater until the evidence I accumulated convinced me
that it was going to kill me so I changed positions. The evidence is
very good for my position -- and I'll gladly present as much of it as
people care to read. Eric has contended that "Vegetarianism in the
modern West has almost nothing to do with even the little we know
about nutrition." Well, we know a good deal about nutrition, Eric, in
spite of your claims to the contrary -- and veganism is perfectly
consistant with what we know, which is that you should follow a diet
with as low a fat intake as is feasable, a high fiber content, and a
wide variety of plant foods in your diet. A "religious movement",
which you contend that this is, wouldn't have as much evidence as we
do. I have already commented on your notion that I and other
health-conscious people who have switched to a vegan diet are ascetics
-- and anyone who doesn't believe me is free to accompany me to a
seven course vegan tasting menu at a top restaurant.

Perry Metzger

