the only thing that people even remotely believe to be missing is B12.
There aren't any studies that show that vegans get low in B12, mind
you -- it just doesn't appear in obvious places in a vegan's diet. (It
might be that peoples such as the Jains end up getting their B12 from
the occassional insect that wanders into the cooking pot -- you need
very very little of it.) I supplement B12 just in case -- but I don't
actually know that its necessary.

> So, I eat veggies, grains, meat, and dairy, to get a general spread of 
> such substances, while avoiding particular things in each category which have
> demonstrated negative effects. 

Well, I'll say this much about dairy: until a mutation appeared in
human digestive systems a mere few thousand years ago, adult humans
were unable to digest dairy, and, indeed, most people around the world
are unable to digest it TO THIS DAY. Caucasians happen to usually have
the mutation, but most people don't. This makes it highly unlikely
that you really need dairy to remain healthy. I won't even get into
the question of whether human digestive tracts are built to consume
meat on a regular basis -- I'd say that the evidence is that they
aren't, but thats much more contraversial than the fact that for most
humans, milk digestion is possible only in early childhood.

Perry

