else. I re-read it for fun at work and was amazed at the synchronicity
since it contained information on stuff I was seriously studying:
Chaitin, UTM's, info theory etc.

At someone's suggestion, I am going to re-open the Chaitin can of worms.

In the previous discussion, I remained quiet. I took the time to do some
research and have been well-rewarded. I have just two points:

- Popularizers (and even Chaitin) are guilty of a certain hyperbole. Instead
  of saying 'arithmetic is random,' it is more accurate to say that beyond
   a certain level of complexity, it is indeterminate. I.e. 2 + 2 always
  equals 4, but beyond certain level of complexity, one cannot make
  statements about x + y, where x,y exceed the complexity of our own
   personal UTMs and that of arithmetic.

- I found this experience to be incredibly invigorating. Casti and others
  seem to find a certain pessimism at the idea that our knowledge is and
  always will be limited. I thought of my P.Chem and Stat Phys experiences
  and was exhilarated. Particle numbers of the magnitude of Avrogadro's
  number are way beyond the capacity of our personal UTM's. Yet, we can
  make amazing accurate statements about them despite not knowing or being
  able to know absolute description of the system.

I apologize if the above was redundant or belated to anyone.

